  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions:\n\nThe strong low level jet from the last night has helped to bring \nsome scattered showers across the Northland as a ribbon of warm air \nadvection between 925-850mb pivots over the region. This activity \ncontinues to slide northeast largely over Lake Superior and portions \nof the Arrowhead. \n\nThis afternoon and evening:\n\nA surface low pressure over SD is set to swing northeast through the \nNorthland helping to produce favorable conditions for severe weather \nthis afternoon and evening. Strong southerly flow can already be \nobserved across the Upper Midwest with gusts in excess of 25 mph \nadvecting higher dewpoints in from the south. Model soundings \nindicate a very unstable air mass will be over the region by the \nafternoon with MLCAPE on the order of 1500+ J/kg. There is a subtle \ncap seen in several models between 800-750mb. So the question will \nbe whether we can generate enough surface heating to overcome \nthis cap in the afternoon. If we can manage to break the cap \nthen storms will have a very favorable environment to grow in. \nMid level lapse rates are quite steep aiding in air parcel \nacceleration leading to rapid storm growth to produce large hail\nand damaging winds. These discrete cells would have access to \nsome favorable helicity with decent curvature in the hodograph \nindicative of a tornado threat. Overall the area most favored \nfor this development would be from the Brainerd Lakes region \ninto the Twin Ports and up through northern MN. Best timing for \nthis set up would be 3PM to 6PM. However, confidence in this \nsolution is not overly high, especially with the added cirrus \nclouds rolling in from the west. But given the favorable \nparameter space for storms it can't be ignored.\n\nThis evening is when the better chances for more widespread showers \nand storms overtake the Northland. Synoptic forcing increases as an \nupper level shortwave moves in tandem with the surface low into \nwestern MN. There is good agreement amongst the high res guidance \nfor an expansion of showers and storms across the Red River Valley \nand through northern MN beginning at 6 PM and onwards into the \nevening. Brief discrete storm mode will be possible initially, but \nthe mean wind flow is more parallel to the boundary which will lead \nto congealing of storms as we quickly move to a more linear \nmode. Damaging winds and some large hail will become the primary\nthreats at this time. Severe potential looks to taper off \naround 10 PM with showers and storms lingering through the \novernight period. Additionally, heavy rain threat may also come \ninto play. PWATs are forecasted to be around 1.50-1.60\" which is\nnear the 90th percentile of climatology. If we get some \ntraining storms the HREF LPMM is highlighting some swaths of 3+ \ninches possible. Not expecting this to be widespread but \nsomething to monitor, current 3hr flash flood guidance is \nlargely over 1.5\" across northern MN. \n\nThe weekend:\n\nAfter today's system moves through we will see a quiet and cool \nweekend. Surface high pressure will be moving from the Canadian \nPrairies down towards the Great Lakes. Highs will be in the 60s and \nlow 70s on Saturday with even cooler temps on Sunday forecasted\nto stay in the 60s. Clear skies on Sunday night may prompt some\nvery chilly overnight lows. Opted to nudge our temperatures \ndown into the mid 30s. Patchy frost may develop and if \ntemperatures continue to trend cooler a Frost Advisory may be \nneeded. \n\nNext Week:\n\nThe cool temperatures look to persist through much of the week \nbefore we start to warm back up into the low 70s on Friday. Rain \nchances return Monday as a vertically stacked low moves across \nManitoba into Ontario. This system looks to have an impressively \nstrong upper level jet associated with it promoting a highly \nsheared environment. The region is lacking in the instability \ndepartment with low MUCAPE forecasted. Overall expecting this to \nlargely manifest as rain showers with an occasional rumble of \nthunder.",
